The present theorem supplies such a test over an infinitesimal coefficient
ring. It is deliberately algebraic: it isolates the curved-transfer issue from
positivity, continuum analysis, and physical selection.

## 2. Exact dual-number source

Let

```text
D=Q[epsilon]/(epsilon^2).
```

On the graded free module `S=D^(2|1)`, use basis parities `(0,1,0)` and define
the odd endomorphism

```text
q=E_10+epsilon E_21.
```

Then

```text
F=q^2=epsilon E_20,
q^3=0,
F^2=0.
```

The curvature is not central. For the even idempotent `e=E_00`,

```text
[F,e]=F,
{F,e}=F.
```

Thus both the Lie and Jordan shadows are nonzero.

## 3. Curved cyclic matrix algebra

Let `B=End_D(S)` with its matrix-superalgebra grading and define

```text
d_q(X)=qX-(-1)^|X|Xq.
```

### Theorem 3.1

On every homogeneous `X,Y in B`,

```text
d_q^2(X)=F X-X F,
d_q(F)=0,
d_q(XY)=d_q(X)Y+(-1)^|X|X d_q(Y).
```

### Proof

Expand the two applications of `d_q`. The mixed terms cancel and the two
remaining terms are left and right multiplication by `q^2=F`. The Bianchi
identity follows from `qF=Fq=q^3=0`; the derivation identity is the usual graded
commutator rule. QED.

The coefficient-of-`epsilon` supertrace

```text
tau(X)=coeff_epsilon Str(X)
```

defines a nondegenerate graded-cyclic pairing on `B`. The verifier checks its
rank on all 18 rational basis directions and checks cyclicity and compatibility
with `d_q` on every homogeneous basis pair.

## 4. Why the curved series terminates

Every occurrence of `F` carries one factor of `epsilon`. Hence, for every
`X in B`,

```text
F X F=0.
```

More generally, every product or transfer tree containing at least two
curvature leaves vanishes. At any fixed arity, the curved expansion therefore
has only the zero-curvature and one-curvature sectors. There is no hidden
infinite formal sum.

This is stronger than checking a small numerical residual: it is an exact
nilpotent-filtration certificate.

